'''belligerate†''' (be-lij′ẹ-rāt), ''v. i.'' [< L. ''belligeratus'', pp. of ''belligerare'', wage war, < ''belliger'', waging war, < ''bellum'', war, + ''gerere'', carry: see ''gest'', ''jest''. Cf. ''belligerent''.] To make war. ''Cockeram.''

'''belligerence''' (be-lij′ẹ-re̤ns), ''n.'' [< ''belligerent'': see ''-ence''.] The act of carrying on war; warfare.

{{smaller block|Merely diplomatic peace, which is honeycombed with suspicion, . . . bristles with the apparatus and establishments of war on a scale far beyond what was formerly required for actual ''belligerence.''
{{right|''Gladstone'', Gleanings, I. 67.}}}}

'''belligerency''' (be-lij′ẹ-re̤n-si), ''n.'' [< ''belligerent'': see ''-ency''.] Position or status as a belligerent; the state of being actually engaged in war.

{{smaller block|They were acting for a Government whose ''belligerency'' had been recognized. ''Soley'', Blockade and Cruisers, p. 224.}}

{{smaller block|I cannot conceive of the existence of any neutral duties when no war exists. Neutrality ''ex vi termini'' implies ''belligerency;'' and a breach of neutrality can only occur with regard to a matter arising during a war.
{{right|''N. A. Rev.'', CXXVII. 39.}}}}

'''belligerent''' (be-lij′ẹ-re̤nt), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Earlier ''belligerant'', < F. ''belligérant'', < L. ''belligeran(t-)s'', ppr. of ''belligerare'', wage war: see ''belligerate''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Warlike; given to waging war; characterized by a tendency to wage or carry on war.

{{smaller block|History teaches that the nations possessing the greatest armaments have always been the most ''belligerent.''
{{right|''Sumner'', Orations, I. 97.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
'''2.''' Of warlike character; constituting or tending to an infraction of peace: as, a ''belligerent'' tone of debate.
{{smaller block|Justice requires that we should commit no ''belligerent'' act not founded in strict right as sanctioned by public law.
{{right|''Lincoln'', in Raymond, p. 167.}}}}
'''3.''' Actually engaged in war: as, the ''belligerent'' powers.—'''4.''' Pertaining to war, or to those engaged in war: as, ''belligerent'' rights, etc.

'''II.''' ''n.'' A nation, power, or state carrying on war; also, a person engaged in fighting.
{{smaller block|The position of neutrals in relation to ''belligerents'' is exactly ascertained.
{{right|''London Times.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The possibility of intercourse in war depends on the confidence which the ''belligerents'' repose in each other’s good faith; and this confidence, on the unchangeable sacredness of truth.
{{right|''Woolsey'', Introd. to Inter. Law, § 249.}}}}
{{smaller block|The rebel Poles had never risen to the rank of ''belligerents.''
{{right|''Lowe'', Bismarck, I. 309.}}}}

'''belligerous†''' (be-lij′e̤-rus), ''a.'' [< L. ''belliger'', waging war, < ''bellum'', war, + ''gerere'', carry on.] Same as ''belligerent''. ''Bailey.''

'''belling<sup>1</sup>''' (bel′ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''bell<sup>1</sup>'', ''v.''] In submarine operations, the use of the diving-bell.

